# Naming convention

The names below are rules and part of the public API (invariant 14). The full inventory of exports, options, flags and reasons is on the pages in the table at the end; read the owning page before adding or renaming a public identifier, option, flag, reason or wire field.

- The brand is the noun. Type `PermDock`, instance `permdock`, factory `createPermDock`. Every server and agent adapter exports `createPermDock`; the import path names the framework (`permdock/next`, `permdock/hono`). Never `NextDock`, `HonoDock`, `dock`, `ability`, `can` as a definer, or `$`-prefixed members.
- Generics follow the instance: a factory that hands out an instance takes `Policy<TUser, TPrincipal, V>` and types it `PermDock<V>` everywhere it reaches user code (the `<Adapter>PermDock<V>` return type, request and context types such as `PermDockRequest<…, V>`, `ConvexPermDockCtx<TCtx, V>`, middleware results, `PdpPermDock<V>`). An adapter whose framework cannot carry a type into the handler offers a typed `withPermDock(handler)` wrapper instead of augmenting the framework's types. Each such adapter has a `tests/<adapter>/vocabulary.test-d.ts` over `tests/fixtures/vocabulary.ts`. Adapters that hand out no instance (`ai-sdk`, `mcp`, `a2a`, `authzen`, `openapi`, `ssf`) and those whose framework erases it (`nest` parameter decorators, `trpc`'s `AnyTRPCMiddlewareFunction`) take `V` only through the policy argument.
- UI: `permdock/react` exports `PermDockProvider`, `usePermDock`, `usePermission`, `<Protected>` and the `use*` hooks; servers use the async `getPermDock` / `getPermission`. Vue uses `use*` composables, Svelte `setPermDock` / `getPermDock` plus readable stores, Solid accessors. `permdock/react-native` takes a required `subjectId` and names helpers by what they wrap or do: `secureStoreStorage`, `mmkvStorage`, `appStateForeground`, `netInfoOnline`, `usePermissionGuard`, `useSnapshotReady`, `powersyncSource`. Never a CASL-style `<Can>`; no components beyond `<Protected>` and the `<PermissionBoundary>` error boundary in `permdock/next/client`, `permdock/vue`, `permdock/svelte` and `permdock/solid` (with `usePermissionBoundary` outside Svelte, where the snippet receives the state). The boundary renders a fallback for a thrown PermDock error and never gates; its props are `denied` and `approval`. `PermDockDeniedError` and `PermDockApprovalRequiredError` carry a stable `digest` (`PERMDOCK_DENIED;<permission>`, `PERMDOCK_APPROVAL_REQUIRED;<permission>;<token>`), read with `parsePermDockDigest`.
- Subjects: every provider is `subjectFrom<Source>`, satisfies `SubjectResolver`, returns a `Subject` and never throws; each exports a `<Provider>Principal` type and a `schema` option. Types come from `PrincipalOf` / `SubjectOf`; never `declare module` augmentation or a `$Infer` accessor.
- Scopes: `definePolicy({ scopes })` declares named scopes in order, each `{ key, within? }`, one tree rooted at the first; `tenant` / `team` alias the first two. A `Membership` is `{ scope, id, within }` or `{ on }`; a role applies only at its own scope (no cascade), and RLS helpers are `permitted_<scope>_ids(p_grant)` plus the membership-only `member_<scope>_ids()`, and on Supabase `member_<scope>_ids_for(p_user)` (executable by `supabase_auth_admin` only, for `supabase.hook.claims` functions) and `permdock_user_id()`, the one subject read of every generated helper and policy (`auth.uid()`, null for an empty `sub`); in `database` mode `permdock_has_for(p_user, p_grant)` and `permitted_<scope>_ids_for(p_user, p_grant)` answer for a named user and no client role may execute them; `permdock_has_permission(p_permission)`, `permitted_<scope>_ids_by_permission(p_permission)` and `grant_keys(p_permission, p_scope, p_effect)` take a permission key, never a `#n` grant key; `permdock_role_permissions(p_role, p_scope, p_tenant, p_scope_id)` and `permdock_permission_keys()` (or `(p_scope)` for the keys roles hold on one scope) read a role's keys and the catalog for role editors, executable by `authenticated`, and `permdock_trusted_role_permissions` reads them for server code, executable by no client role and by the `rls.trustedReaders` roles; `permitted_<scope>_permission_keys(p_id[, p_keys])` lists the keys held on one instance in one set-based query (`_for(p_user, p_id[, p_keys])` in `database` mode). Never a hard-coded tenant/team kind in new code.
- Links: a share link is a `Capability` (v1) signed as `permdock-capability+jwt` by `signCapability` and resolved by `subjectFromCapability` into a `kind: 'link'` principal with one `{ on, roles, via: 'link' }` membership; it never holds a scope or global role. `holder: 'key'` stays reserved. RLS reaches links only through `exchangeCapability` (a short-lived `anon` token, never `service_role`, no `sub`) and `permdock_capability_ids` under `--capabilities` ([link capabilities](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/capabilities.mdx)).
- Keys: an API key is an opaque `pdk_<id>_<secret>` whose SHA-256 hash the app stores next to a `Credential` (v1); `subjectFromApiKey(options)` in `permdock/server` is the only resolver and returns a `SubjectResolver`. A `user` key is its owner's live rights narrowed through `delegation` (scopes, RFC 9396 entries with `identifier`), and an optional `tenant` holds it to the owner's memberships there; a `service` key is a `kind: 'service'` principal with one `{ tenant, roles, via: 'credential' }` membership, created by `decideCredential` only within the creator's `assignableRoles` / `assignablePermissions` (`exceeds-creator`). Tenant rules come from a `SettingsSource` (`credentials: { maxTtl, kinds, approval, allowNoExpiry }`); a key without expiry needs `allowNoExpiry`. The key itself never reaches RLS and is not a capability; `rls.apiKeys` reads a backend-set claim (`api_key`: `scopes`, `tenant`, `roles`) through `permdock_api_key_allows`, a ceiling and never a grant beyond the key's roles ([API keys](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/credentials.mdx)).
- better-supabase: `permdock/better-supabase` fills better-supabase's runtime slots and names them after the slot, not the brand: `authorizationProvider` (its `authorization` config), `bucketPolicy` and `topicPolicy` (its storage and realtime access policies, taking `Permission` references), `apiKeyVerifier` (a `CredentialVerifier` over its API keys block), `apiKeyClaimOptions` (its `api_key` claim settings from `rls.apiKeys`), `toolPolicy` (its `createMcp` `authorize` and `visible` hooks), `credentialGuard` (a `CredentialProvider` that PermDock guards) and `subjectFromBetterSupabase`. `apiKeyVerifier` shares its name with the one in `permdock/server` on purpose: both fill the same `CredentialVerifier` slot over a different key store. `permdock/supabase` stays plain Supabase and never names better-supabase ([better-supabase](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/better-supabase.mdx)).
- Field security: RLS is row-level unless `rls generate --fields views` (`rls.fields: 'views'`) emits `<table>_visible`, a `security_invoker` view whose restricted columns are `case when <permitted> then col end` over the row policy's helper calls; `--revoke-columns` (`rls.revokeColumns`) keeps only unrestricted columns and the key readable on the table and reads the rest through the owner-rights `<table>_visible_fields` companion (joined on `permdock_key`, marked `permdock:field-companion`). Never Postgres column privileges per app role and never a permission per field. `rls import` returns `fieldViews`, `rlsParity({ fieldViews })` compares with `pick`, doctor PD030 ([field security](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/rls.mdx)).
- Graph: a resource's `parent` may name itself, `restricted` names a boolean column or `{ field, stops }` (`'parent'` and link names it closes; a closed link also checks the rows above through `permdock_restricted_<resource>`), and relations are `{ field, memberOf? }`, `{ edge, object?, subject?, expiresAt? }` or `{ principal, period? }`. `relation(resource, name, { through: 'parent', depth })` walks the chain (default 16, at most 32) through a `RelationSource` (`ancestors`, `related`; `memoryRelations`, `testRelationSource`) passed as `relations`, cached per instance; `loadRelations` fills the cache and `whoCan` lists holders with `complete`, never granting. Reasons `relation-depth` and `relation-unavailable`. Snapshots carry no graph (graph grants are `portable: false`). RLS: `permdock_closure` with statement triggers, `permitted_<resource>_ids(relation)` (resource in snake_case), `rls.rowHelpers` with `permitted_<resource>_rows(permission)`, `_rows_for(p_user, permission, p_claims)` and the row-valued `permitted_<resource>_row(p_row, permission)`, `inherit(permission, { through })` as a grantee read through `RelationSource.row`, `rls verify --tree`, doctor PD031 / PD032 ([relationships](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/relationships.mdx)).
- Options, not exports: `webBotAuth`, `store` (`ApprovalStore`), `sink` (`DecisionSink`), `source` (`SnapshotSource`, on `permdock/react-native`), `policies` (`PolicySource`), `verifier` (`TokenVerifier` or `CredentialVerifier`), `signer`, `memberships` (one `MembershipSource` or an array, composed), `customRoles`, `entitlements` (`EntitlementSource`), `settings` (`SettingsSource`), `relations` (`RelationSource`), `limits`. Every store, sink and source interface has an in-package `memory*` default; every extension interface has a `test<Interface>` runner in `permdock/testing`.
- Definitions and policy: `definePermissions`, `resource`, `definePolicy`, `role`, `allow`, `deny` and the other plain functions on `concepts/policies.mdx`; `crud` / `readable` / `writable` are option factories for `resource()`. Never a fluent policy builder.
- Errors: `PermDockDeniedError`, `PermDockApprovalRequiredError`, `PermDockValidationError`; Web Bot Auth failures throw `InvalidSignatureError` and never become an anonymous actor.
- Denial reasons are a closed list (`concepts/decisions.mdx`). A boundary failure is `validation`, never `invalid-resource`; an undeclared role is `unknown-role`.
- Never `SnapshotV2` (the major is the `v` field), never `@permdock/cloud` (it is the `permdock/cloud` entry), never a per-vendor entry such as `permdock/hey-api`, `permdock/scalar`, `permdock/mintlify` or `permdock/chat` ([adapters](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/index.mdx)). `with*` belongs to the Supabase middleware pipeline, to `withSubject`, the transaction helper of `permdock/drizzle`, `permdock/kysely` and `permdock/prisma`, and to `withOtel`, the instance wrapper of `permdock/otel`. `permdock/terminal` is for consumers' CLIs and is not the `permdock` binary or `permdock/cli`; never a scoped `@permdock/*` package (`permdock`, owned by the `scaledockhq` npm organisation, is the only published name); the build hooks `createPermDockPlugin` / `createPermDockUnplugin` collect only.
- OpenAPI output: only `x-permdock-*`, registered `x-oai-*` names, and the opt-in `x-badges` hint. Never another party's namespace (`x-scalar-*`, `x-speakeasy-*`, `x-fern-*`, `x-gnap`, gateway namespaces such as `x-amazon-apigateway-*`) and never `targetFormat`. Draft constructs (OpenAPI 3.3 profiles, Overlay 1.2) always ship next to a stable twin with the pin in `x-permdock-catalog.drafts`.
- A field a specification already names keeps the spec's name; add a row to the "Spec names" table on `getting-started/naming.mdx` before adding it.
- Approvals refuse the requester: `distinct` defaults to `true`, `distinct: false` is the per-grant opt-out (doctor PD024), and `requireDistinctApprover` is a handler-wide floor over it. Never an `allowSelf` flag.
- Approvers: `user(id)` for one person, `relation()` checked only through verdict facts from `approverRelations`, `holder(permission)` checked only through `verdict.permissions` from `approverPermissions`, `anyOf(...)` for any-of groups (a list or `allOf(...)` is all-of), `mode` `'any' | 'all' | 'sequential'` with `stages`. Data rules are an `ApprovalPolicySource` (`approvalPolicies`), which only adds stages and fails closed. Never `steps`, `chain` or `approvalRules`.
- Renames: `definePermissions(tree, { renamed })` maps former keys to current ones; the catalog field is `renamedFrom`, the diff kinds are `renamed` and `alias-removed`. Never `aliases` or `legacyKeys`.
- Approvals resume one call: `ApprovalStore.consume` sets `consumedAt`. The HTTP resume header is `PermDock-Approval` ([approvals](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/approvals.mdx)).
- Snapshots: `snapshotFor` and `mayAccess` are the cache building blocks. PermDock never adds `'use cache'` or calls `headers()` / `cookies()` itself; `getSnapshot` on the `permdock/next` factory calls `cacheLife` and `cacheTag` only inside the app's own `'use cache: private'` scope; `tenants: 'all'` is opt-in ([Next.js Cache Components](../../apps/docs/content/docs/guides/next-cache-components.mdx)).
- Custom roles: `scope: 'global'` marks a platform custom role (`RoleSource.globalRoles`), capped by assignable global roles. `CustomRole.grants` entries are `{ permission, effect?, level? }` and carry no condition, approval or limit; `level` names a condition the resource declares in code (`resource(…, { levels })`), and an undeclared one is `unknown-level` and denies. `resolveCustomRole` is the only resolver (evaluation, `snapshot`, `snapshotFor`, `validateCustomRole`, doctor PD023); the ceiling is the code allows of declared `assignable` roles in the role's scope, never hosted grants. `assignablePermissions` / `useAssignablePermissions` sit next to `assignableRoles`; `meta.manageRoles` on a role or permission lifts the held-grant intersection ([custom roles](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/custom-roles.mdx)).
- Ownership: role options `min`, `max`, `transferOnly`, `assigns`, `for` and `meta.audience` (`RoleMeta`); `permdock.decideRoleChange(change)` answers `granted` / `denied` with the closed reasons `last-holder`, `max-holders`, `transfer-only`, `not-assignable-by`, `self-demotion`, `not-allowed-for-membership`, `conflicting-role`, takes the actor from the instance and fails closed without `holders`; a role held through a kind outside `for` is dropped at resolution; an `assigns` graph replaces the held-grant rule for `assignableRoles` and ranks `heldRoles`; `audiences()` / `snapshot.audiences` never grant. SQL objects are `permdock_can_assign`, `permdock_holders_<scope>`, `permdock_transfer_only_<scope>_*` and the `rls.assignments` triggers `permdock_assignment_<table>` on the membership, global-roles and listed tables, with `ownRole: 'refuse'` for the caller's own rows; doctor PD026 and PD064 ([ownership](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/ownership.mdx)).
- Streams and sockets: `connection(...)` returns a frozen `Connection`. `RevocationFeed` (`memoryRevocationFeed()`) ends or revalidates a connection and never grants. `PermDockRevokedError` is only a connection signal. An abort adds no denial reason ([streams](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/streams.mdx)).

